the ConditionsAwareDecisionConditionsMap constructor will also apply this logic:

```go
	// short-circuit case: if no Allow or Deny conditions exist, then evaluating this ConditionsMap can never evaluate to Allow or Deny,
	// but only NoOpinion, regardless of the data. Thus just fold to NoOpinion directly.
	// this upholds the len(denyConditions) != 0 || len(allowConditions) != 0 invariant on the ConditionsMap.
	if len(denyConditions) == 0 && len(allowConditions) == 0 {
		return ConditionsAwareDecisionNoOpinion("", nil)
	}
```

Can we drop this special case here and lean on the ConditionsAwareDecisionConditionsMap constructor? I think that would make ~all the cases symmetrical (except for being able to return aggregated errors in the allow case because we're not trying to avoid a 500).

In some ways, the extensive commenting is instructive, but in some ways, it obscures the flow of this function.

I ended up stripping out comments and consolidating lines / variable names to be able to see the structure of the three refining cases is ~identical:

```go
	if len(c.denyConditions) != 0 {
		appliedReasons, errors, unevaluated := conditionsToAppliedErroredUnevaluated(c.DenyConditions(), evalCond, "Deny", "denied the request")
		if len(appliedReasons) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionDeny(strings.Join(appliedReasons, ", "), nil)
		}
		if len(errors) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionDeny("one or more conditional evaluation errors occurred", utilerrors.NewAggregate(errors))
		}
		if len(unevaluated) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionConditionsMap(unevaluated, deepCopyConditions(c.noOpinionConditions), deepCopyConditions(c.allowConditions))
		}
	}

	// If we got here, all Deny conditions could be evaluated, and evaluated to false, nil

	if len(c.noOpinionConditions) != 0 {
		appliedReasons, errors, unevaluated := conditionsToAppliedErroredUnevaluated(c.NoOpinionConditions(), evalCond, "NoOpinion", "evaluated to NoOpinion")
		if len(appliedReasons) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionNoOpinion(strings.Join(appliedReasons, ", "), nil)
		}
		if len(errors) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionNoOpinion("one or more conditional evaluation errors occurred", utilerrors.NewAggregate(errors))
		}
		if len(unevaluated) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionConditionsMap(nil, unevaluated, deepCopyConditions(c.allowConditions))
		}
	}

	// If we got here, all Deny and NoOpinion conditions could be evaluated, and evaluated to false, nil

	if len(c.allowConditions) != 0 {
		appliedReasons, errors, unevaluated := conditionsToAppliedErroredUnevaluated(c.AllowConditions(), evalCond, "Allow", "allowed the request")
		if len(appliedReasons) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionAllow(strings.Join(appliedReasons, ", "), utilerrors.NewAggregate(errors))
		}
		if len(errors) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionNoOpinion("one or more conditional evaluation errors occurred", utilerrors.NewAggregate(errors))
		}
		if len(unevaluated) != 0 {
			return ConditionsAwareDecisionConditionsMap(nil, nil, unevaluated)
		}
	}

	// All conditions evaluated to false. This means a simple default NoOpinion.
	return ConditionsAwareDecisionNoOpinion("no conditions matched", nil)
```

